[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Core Java Keywords: this, super, final, static, abstract, volatile, transient, synchronized"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial explores eight essential keywords in Core Java: `this`, `super`, `final`, `static`, `abstract`, `volatile`, `transient`, and `synchronized`. Understanding these keywords is crucial for writing robust, efficient, and maintainable Java code. Each keyword serves a specific purpose in defining the behavior and characteristics of classes, methods, and variables."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. The 'this' Keyword"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `this` keyword is a reference to the current object. It is used to differentiate between instance variables and local variables with the same name, to call one constructor from another within the same class (constructor chaining), and to return the current object from a method."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1.1 Differentiating Instance and Local Variables"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"public class Rectangle {
    private int width;
    private int height;

    public Rectangle(int width, int height) {
        this.width = width; // 'this.width' refers to the instance variable
        this.height = height; // 'width' and 'height' are local variables
    }

    public int getWidth() {
        return width;
    }

    public int getHeight() {
        return height;
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the above example, `this.width` refers to the instance variable `width` of the `Rectangle` class, while `width` (without `this.`) refers to the local variable `width` passed as an argument to the constructor. This distinction is essential when the names of instance and local variables collide."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"1.2 Constructor Chaining"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public class Circle {
    private double radius;
    private String color;

    public Circle() {
        this(1.0, "red"); // Calls the constructor with two arguments
    }

    public Circle(double radius) {
        this(radius, "red"); // Calls the constructor with two arguments
    }

    public Circle(double radius, String color) {
        this.radius = radius;
        this.color = color;
    }

    public double getRadius() {
        return radius;
    }

    public String getColor() {
        return color;
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the `this()` keyword is used to call one constructor from another within the same class. This is useful for avoiding code duplication and ensuring that common initialization logic is executed regardless of which constructor is used."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"1.3 Returning the Current Object"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public class Person {
    private String name;

    public Person setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        return this; // Returns the current Person object
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person person = new Person();
        person.setName("Alice").setName("Bob"); // Method chaining
        System.out.println(person.getName()); // Output: Bob
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Returning `this` allows for method chaining, where multiple methods can be called sequentially on the same object. This can improve code readability and conciseness."},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"2. The 'super' Keyword"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `super` keyword is used to refer to the superclass (parent class) of the current class. It is used to access superclass members (methods and variables) and to call the superclass constructor."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"2.1 Accessing Superclass Members"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
    String color = "white";

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating...");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    String color = "black";

    void displayColor() {
        System.out.println("Dog color: " + color); // Dog's color
        System.out.println("Animal color: " + super.color); // Animal's color
    }

    void eat() {
        super.eat(); // Calls the eat() method of the Animal class
        System.out.println("Dog is eating...");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog dog = new Dog();
        dog.displayColor();
        dog.eat();
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `super.color` accesses the `color` variable of the `Animal` class, and `super.eat()` calls the `eat()` method of the `Animal` class. This allows the `Dog` class to extend and modify the behavior of the `Animal` class."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"2.2 Calling Superclass Constructor"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"class Vehicle {
    String modelName;

    Vehicle(String modelName) {
        this.modelName = modelName;
        System.out.println("Vehicle Constructor");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    String carType;

    Car(String modelName, String carType) {
        super(modelName); // Calls the constructor of the Vehicle class
        this.carType = carType;
        System.out.println("Car Constructor");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car car = new Car("Model X", "Sedan");
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `super(modelName)` call in the `Car` constructor invokes the constructor of the `Vehicle` class, passing the `modelName` argument. This ensures that the superclass is properly initialized before the subclass's initialization logic is executed.  It's important to note that the call to `super()` must be the first statement in the constructor."},{"order":22,"type":"heading","text":"3. The 'final' Keyword"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `final` keyword is used to restrict modification of a variable, method, or class. It has different meanings depending on where it's used:"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`final` variable: The value of the variable cannot be changed once it's initialized. It becomes a constant."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`final` method: The method cannot be overridden in a subclass."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`final` class: The class cannot be subclassed (extended)."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"3.1 Final Variable"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"public class Constants {
    public static final int MAX_VALUE = 100;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // MAX_VALUE = 200; // Compilation error: cannot assign a value to final variable MAX_VALUE
        System.out.println(MAX_VALUE);
    }
}"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once `MAX_VALUE` is initialized to 100, its value cannot be changed. Attempting to assign a new value will result in a compilation error."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"3.2 Final Method"},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"class Parent {
    public final void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent display");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    // public void display() { // Compilation error: cannot override the final method from Parent
    //     System.out.println("Child display");
    // }
}
"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `display()` method in the `Parent` class is declared as `final`, so it cannot be overridden in the `Child` class. Attempting to do so will result in a compilation error."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"3.3 Final Class"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"final class ImmutableClass {
    private final int value;

    public ImmutableClass(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public int getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

// class SubClass extends ImmutableClass { // Compilation error: cannot inherit from final ImmutableClass
//
// }
"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `ImmutableClass` is declared as `final`, so it cannot be subclassed. This prevents other classes from extending its functionality and potentially modifying its behavior.  This is often used to create immutable objects."},{"order":36,"type":"heading","text":"4. The 'static' Keyword"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `static` keyword is used to create members (variables and methods) that belong to the class itself, rather than to individual instances of the class. Static members are shared by all instances of the class."},{"order":38,"type":"subheading","text":"4.1 Static Variable"},{"order":39,"type":"code","text":"class Counter {
    static int count = 0; // Static variable

    Counter() {
        count++;
        System.out.println(count);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Counter c1 = new Counter(); // Output: 1
        Counter c2 = new Counter(); // Output: 2
        Counter c3 = new Counter(); // Output: 3
    }
}"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `count` variable is static, so it is shared by all instances of the `Counter` class. Each time a `Counter` object is created, the `count` variable is incremented, and the new value is printed.  There is only one copy of the `count` variable."},{"order":41,"type":"subheading","text":"4.2 Static Method"},{"order":42,"type":"code","text":"class MathUtils {
    public static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int sum = MathUtils.add(5, 3); // Calling the static method using the class name
        System.out.println(sum); // Output: 8
    }
}"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `add()` method is static, so it can be called directly using the class name (`MathUtils.add()`) without creating an instance of the class. Static methods are often used for utility functions that don't depend on the state of a particular object."},{"order":44,"type":"subheading","text":"4.3 Static Block"},{"order":45,"type":"code","text":"public class StaticBlockExample {
    static int num;
    static String str;

    // Static block to initialize static variables
    static {
        num = 100;
        str = "Static block initialization";
        System.out.println("Static block executed");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Value of num: " + num);
        System.out.println("Value of str: " + str);
    }
}"},{"order":46,"type":"paragraph","text":"A static block is a block of code that is executed only once when the class is loaded into memory. It is used to initialize static variables or perform other one-time initialization tasks. The static block is executed before the main method."},{"order":47,"type":"heading","text":"5. The 'abstract' Keyword"},{"order":48,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `abstract` keyword is used to declare abstract classes and abstract methods. An abstract class cannot be instantiated, and it may contain abstract methods (methods without an implementation). Subclasses of an abstract class must provide implementations for all abstract methods."},{"order":49,"type":"subheading","text":"5.1 Abstract Class"},{"order":50,"type":"code","text":"abstract class Shape {
    abstract double getArea(); // Abstract method

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Shape display");
    }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    double getArea() { // Implementing the abstract method
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape shape = new Circle(5.0); // Valid: Circle is a concrete subclass of Shape
        System.out.println("Area: " + shape.getArea());
        shape.display();
    }
}
"},{"order":51,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Shape` class is declared as `abstract`, so it cannot be instantiated directly. It contains an abstract method `getArea()`, which must be implemented by any concrete subclass, such as `Circle`. The `@Override` annotation is used to indicate that the `getArea()` method in the `Circle` class is overriding the abstract method in the `Shape` class."},{"order":52,"type":"heading","text":"6. The 'volatile' Keyword"},{"order":53,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `volatile` keyword is used to indicate that a variable's value may be changed by multiple threads. It ensures that the variable's value is always read from and written to main memory, rather than from a thread's local cache. This helps prevent data inconsistencies in multithreaded environments."},{"order":54,"type":"code","text":"public class VolatileExample {
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public void start() {
        new Thread(() -> {
            while (running) {
                // Do some work
                System.out.println("Running...");
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            }
            System.out.println("Stopped");
        }).start();
    }

    public void stop() {
        running = false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        VolatileExample example = new VolatileExample();
        example.start();
        Thread.sleep(1000);
        example.stop();
    }
}"},{"order":55,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `running` variable is declared as `volatile`. This ensures that when the `stop()` method is called from the main thread, the change to `running` is immediately visible to the thread executing the `start()` method. Without `volatile`, the thread might continue running indefinitely because it's reading the value of `running` from its local cache."},{"order":56,"type":"heading","text":"7. The 'transient' Keyword"},{"order":57,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `transient` keyword is used to indicate that a variable should not be serialized. Serialization is the process of converting an object's state to a byte stream, which can then be stored or transmitted. Transient variables are excluded from this process."},{"order":58,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.*;

public class TransientExample implements Serializable {
    private String name;
    private transient String password; // This field will not be serialized

    public TransientExample(String name, String password) {
        this.name = name;
        this.password = password;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public String getPassword() {
        return password;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransientExample user = new TransientExample("Alice", "secret");

        // Serialize the object
        try (ObjectOutputStream oos = new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("user.ser"))) {
            oos.writeObject(user);
            System.out.println("Object serialized");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        // Deserialize the object
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream("user.ser"))) {
            TransientExample deserializedUser = (TransientExample) ois.readObject();
            System.out.println("Object deserialized");
            System.out.println("Name: " + deserializedUser.getName());
            System.out.println("Password: " + deserializedUser.getPassword()); // Password will be null
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":59,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `password` variable is declared as `transient`. When the `TransientExample` object is serialized, the value of `password` is not included in the byte stream. When the object is deserialized, the `password` variable will be `null`. This is often used to protect sensitive information, such as passwords, from being stored in serialized form."},{"order":60,"type":"heading","text":"8. The 'synchronized' Keyword"},{"order":61,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `synchronized` keyword is used to control access to shared resources in multithreaded environments. It ensures that only one thread can execute a synchronized block or method at a time, preventing race conditions and data corruption. It provides a lock on the object or class being synchronized."},{"order":62,"type":"subheading","text":"8.1 Synchronized Method"},{"order":63,"type":"code","text":"public class SynchronizedCounter {
    private int count = 0;

    public synchronized void increment() {
        count++;
    }

    public synchronized int getCount() {
        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SynchronizedCounter counter = new SynchronizedCounter();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                counter.increment();
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                counter.increment();
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println("Count: " + counter.getCount()); // Expected: 2000
    }
}"},{"order":64,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `increment()` and `getCount()` methods are declared as `synchronized`. This ensures that only one thread can execute either of these methods at a time. When a thread calls `increment()`, it acquires a lock on the `counter` object. Other threads attempting to call `increment()` or `getCount()` will be blocked until the first thread releases the lock. This prevents race conditions and ensures that the `count` variable is updated correctly."},{"order":65,"type":"subheading","text":"8.2 Synchronized Block"},{"order":66,"type":"code","text":"public class SynchronizedBlockExample {
    private int count = 0;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public void increment() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            count++;
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            return count;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SynchronizedBlockExample example = new SynchronizedBlockExample();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                example.increment();
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                example.increment();
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println("Count: " + example.getCount()); // Expected: 2000
    }
}"},{"order":67,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, a synchronized block is used with a dedicated `lock` object. Only the code within the `synchronized(lock)` block is protected by the lock. This allows for finer-grained control over synchronization, as only the critical sections of code need to be synchronized. Using a dedicated lock object is generally preferred over synchronizing on `this` to avoid unintended synchronization issues."}]
